{{also|Aspartame}}
==English==

===Etymology===
Commercial name, from {{m|en|aspartic}}. The ''-ame'' is perhaps because aspartame is an [[amide]].<ref>{{R:Online Etymology Dictionary|pos=n|id=17942}}</ref> Compare {{m|en|aspartamine}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/əˈspɑːteɪm/}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/ˈæspɚteɪm/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-aspartame.opus|Audio (GA)}}
* {{hyphenation|en|aspar|tame|caption=GB}}; {{hyphenation|en|as|par|tame|caption=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|s}}

# {{lb|en|organic compound}} An [[artificial sweetener]], the [[methyl]] [[ester]] of a [[dipeptide]] formed from [[aspartic acid]] and [[phenylalanine]], used in many [[processed]] [[food]]s and [[beverage]]s.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2001|author=C. Keith Conners|title=Feeding the Brain: How Foods Affect Children|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=eqxDoN4KGGcC&pg=PA38&dq=%22Aspartame%22&hl=en&ei=MQCoTfWEIImcvgOXs_WNCg&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=31&ved=0CMUBEOgBMB4#v=onepage&q=%22Aspartame%22&f=false|page=38
|passage=Though reluctant to further expose him to '''aspartame''', I had little trouble persuading Jamie's mother to carry out a double-blind trial in order to more rigorously document her son's aberrant response to '''aspartame'''.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|author=w:J. G. Thirlwell|title=Sieve|album=Damp|year=2006|artist={{w|Foetus (band)|Foetus}}|text=Caramel colors and '''aspartame'''<br>Phosphoric acid, potassium<br>Natural flavors, of course caffeine<br>And now I'm jonesin' for MSG}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007|author=Nina Redman|title=Food Safety: A Reference Handbook|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=D00xd8qNUvYC&pg=PA37&dq=%22Aspartame%22&hl=en&ei=MQCoTfWEIImcvgOXs_WNCg&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=6&ved=0CEgQ6AEwBQ#v=onepage&q=%22Aspartame%22&f=false|page=37
|passage='''Aspartame''', sold under the brand NutraSweet, was discovered accidentally by a scientist at Searle in 1965 who was testing new drugs for gastric ulcers and licked his fingers before picking up a piece of paper.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2008|author=Frances Sienkiewicz Sizer; Eleanor Noss Whitney|title=Nutrition: Concepts and Controversies|volume=10|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=mDhMU0Sv6asC&pg=PA143&dq=%22Aspartame%22&hl=en&ei=MQCoTfWEIImcvgOXs_WNCg&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=40&ved=0CPUBEOgBMCc#v=onepage&q=%22Aspartame%22&f=false|page=143
|passage=In powdered form, '''aspartame''' is mixed with lactose, so a 1-gram packet contains 4 calories.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2023-05-15|author=April Rubin|title=World Health Organization Warns Against Using Artificial Sweeteners|journal=w:The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/05/15/well/eat/sweeteners-weight-loss-who.html|passage=Some examples of the sweeteners include '''aspartame''', saccharin, sucralose and stevia. The W.H.O.’s announcement contradicts previous studies that have said these sweeteners don’t offer any health benefits but also do not cause harm.}}

